<section #l1-aside | `<aside>`>

<sub-section | Specification>

`category` `block-prose` · Layer 1 `<aside>` · JATS `<boxed-text>` · `content` `prose` → `<children>`

| attribute | kind | values / default | notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| `type` | kwarg | `note` `sidebar` `callout` `warning` `tip` `info` `caution` | Optional classification of the aside's role. Affects rendering (callouts get visual emphasis; sidebars get layout treatment). Maps to JATS via boxed-text content-type at export. |
| `title` | kwarg |  | Optional title rendered at the top of the aside (the frameable title-top convention, #31). Lifts to a <title> child tag at the normalize-to-canonical gate; the child form is equivalent. |
| `caption` | kwarg |  | Optional caption rendered at the foot of the aside (frameable caption-bottom convention, #31), with the "Box N." label folded in when numbered. Lifts to a <caption> child tag at the gate. |
| `+numbered` | boolean | default `false` | Whether this aside is numbered. **Off by default** (boxed prose is usually a one-off callout). When +numbered, the aside counts in its OWN "Box N" series (the `box` counter / ref-prefix `box`), not the figure counter. |
| `+border` | boolean | default `true` | Frameable surface (#31). **On by default for boxed prose** — the visual box is the point of a callout / sidebar. Use -border to suppress the outline. |

Produced by: canonical `<aside>`.

<sub-section | Examples>

<code #code:l1-aside-1>
<aside | A side note about the elephant.>
</code>

<minipage #mp:l1-aside-1>
<aside | A side note about the elephant.>
</minipage>

<code #code:l1-aside-2>
<aside type=warning .important | Be careful here.>
</code>

<minipage #mp:l1-aside-2>
<aside type=warning .important | Be careful here.>
</minipage>

<code #code:l1-aside-3>
<aside type=callout |
This is a multi-line callout.

It can contain multiple paragraphs and other content like
<strong | emphasis> and inline references.
>
</code>

<minipage #mp:l1-aside-3>
<aside type=callout |
This is a multi-line callout.

It can contain multiple paragraphs and other content like
<strong | emphasis> and inline references.
>
</minipage>

</section>

<section #l1-blockquote | `<blockquote>`>

<sub-section | Specification>

`category` `block-prose` · Layer 1 `<blockquote>` · JATS `<disp-quote or epigraph>` · `content` `prose` → `<children>`

| attribute | kind | values / default | notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| `cite` | kwarg |  | URL of the source being quoted. Maps to HTML's standard cite attribute on <blockquote>. For inline citation references (citing a bibliography entry), use a <cite> element inside the blockquote instead. |
| `type` | kwarg | `verse` `dialogue` `epigraph` `pullquote` `other` | Optional classification of the quotation's role. Affects styling and may affect JATS export. |

Produced by: canonical `<blockquote>` · markdown `> …` · alias `<quote>`.

<sub-section | Examples>

<code #code:l1-blockquote-1>
<quote | A short quotation.>
</code>

<minipage #mp:l1-blockquote-1>
<quote | A short quotation.>
</minipage>

*The &lt;quote&gt; shorthand expands to &lt;blockquote&gt; at Layer 1.*

<code #code:l1-blockquote-2>
<quote cite=https://example.com/source |
A longer quotation that may contain multiple paragraphs.

The second paragraph of the quotation.
>
</code>

<minipage #mp:l1-blockquote-2>
<quote cite=https://example.com/source |
A longer quotation that may contain multiple paragraphs.

The second paragraph of the quotation.
>
</minipage>

<code #code:l1-blockquote-3>
<quote type=epigraph |
All happy families are alike; each unhappy family is unhappy in its own way.
>
</code>

<minipage #mp:l1-blockquote-3>
<quote type=epigraph |
All happy families are alike; each unhappy family is unhappy in its own way.
>
</minipage>

<code #code:l1-blockquote-4>
<blockquote | Same as <quote> but using the explicit Layer 1 name.>
</code>

*Render pending — the example source has an unescaped `<…>` tag-mention the parser consumes into an empty nested element (issue #277).*

*Authors can also write &lt;blockquote&gt; directly. Both forms produce the same Layer 1 output.*

</section>

<section #l1-dd | `<dd>`>

<sub-section | Specification>

`category` `block-prose` · Layer 1 `<dd>` · JATS `<def>` · `content` `prose` → `<children>`

*No kwargs or boolean flags.*

Produced by: canonical `<dd>`.

<sub-section | Examples>

<code #code:l1-dd-1>
<dd | An academic publishing system built on HTML+CSS+JS.>
</code>

<minipage #mp:l1-dd-1>
<dd | An academic publishing system built on HTML+CSS+JS.>
</minipage>

*A definition-list description. Appears as a child of &lt;dl&gt;, following the term it defines.*

<code #code:l1-dd-2>
<dd |
A multi-paragraph definition.

The second paragraph of the definition.
>
</code>

<minipage #mp:l1-dd-2>
<dd |
A multi-paragraph definition.

The second paragraph of the definition.
>
</minipage>

*Multi-paragraph descriptions are valid — the pipe content's paragraph structure is preserved.*

</section>

<section #l1-details | `<details>`>

<sub-section | Specification>

`category` `block-prose` · Layer 1 `<details>` · JATS `<(no direct JATS counterpart; HTML-native)>` · `content` `structured`

| attribute | kind | values / default | notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| `open` | kwarg | `true` `false` | HTML's open attribute on <details>. When present, the disclosure is expanded by default. Either +open or open=true form works; absence (or -open / open=false) renders collapsed. |

Produced by: canonical `<details>`.

<sub-section | Examples>

<code #code:l1-details-1>
<details>
  <summary | More background>
  Additional context for the curious reader. The summary is the
  visible heading; this body shows when the disclosure is opened.
</details>
</code>

<minipage #mp:l1-details-1>
<details>
  <summary | More background>
  Additional context for the curious reader. The summary is the
  visible heading; this body shows when the disclosure is opened.
</details>
</minipage>

*The canonical shape: a &lt;summary&gt; for the visible heading, followed by the disclosure body. The body is recursively parsed as prose / block content.*

<code #code:l1-details-2>
<details +open>
  <summary | Always-expanded section>
  This disclosure is expanded by default.
</details>
</code>

<minipage #mp:l1-details-2>
<details +open>
  <summary | Always-expanded section>
  This disclosure is expanded by default.
</details>
</minipage>

*The +open boolean kwarg expands the disclosure by default. Maps to HTML's standard open attribute.*

</section>

<section #l1-dl | `<dl>`>

<sub-section | Specification>

`category` `block-prose` · Layer 1 `<dl>` · JATS `<def-list>` · `content` `structured`

*No kwargs or boolean flags.*

Produced by: canonical `<dl>`.

<sub-section | Examples>

<code #code:l1-dl-1>
<dl>
  <dt | enscribe>
  <dd | An academic publishing system built on HTML+CSS+JS.>
  <dt | Layer 1>
  <dd | The canonical semantic HTML vocabulary.>
  <dt | Layer 2>
  <dd | The shorthand authoring syntax that compiles to Layer 1.>
</dl>
</code>

<minipage #mp:l1-dl-1>
<dl>
  <dt | enscribe>
  <dd | An academic publishing system built on HTML+CSS+JS.>
  <dt | Layer 1>
  <dd | The canonical semantic HTML vocabulary.>
  <dt | Layer 2>
  <dd | The shorthand authoring syntax that compiles to Layer 1.>
</dl>
</minipage>

*Long-form &lt;dl&gt; with short-form &lt;dt&gt;/&lt;dd&gt; children. The natural authoring pattern.*

<code #code:l1-dl-2>
<dl .compact>
  <dt | term-1>
  <dd | First definition of term-1.>
  <dd | Second definition of term-1.>
</dl>
</code>

<minipage #mp:l1-dl-2>
<dl .compact>
  <dt | term-1>
  <dd | First definition of term-1.>
  <dd | Second definition of term-1.>
</dl>
</minipage>

*One term with multiple definitions — a valid HTML pattern.*

</section>

<section #l1-dt | `<dt>`>

<sub-section | Specification>

`category` `block-prose` · Layer 1 `<dt>` · JATS `<term>` · `content` `prose` → `<children>`

*No kwargs or boolean flags.*

Produced by: canonical `<dt>`.

<sub-section | Examples>

<code #code:l1-dt-1>
<dt | enscribe>
</code>

<minipage #mp:l1-dt-1>
<dt | enscribe>
</minipage>

*A definition-list term. Appears as a child of &lt;dl&gt;.*

<code #code:l1-dt-2>
<dt | <code | strict-mode>>
</code>

<minipage #mp:l1-dt-2>
<dt | <code | strict-mode>>
</minipage>

*Inline markup in a term. The recursive-content pass parses the pipe content normally.*

</section>

<section #l1-endnotes | `<endnotes>`>

<sub-section | Specification>

`category` `block-prose` · Layer 1 `<endnotes>` · JATS `<fn-group>` · `content` `structured`

*No kwargs or boolean flags.*

Produced by: *structural — emitted by the compiler, not authored directly*.

</section>

<section #l1-glossary | `<glossary>`>

<sub-section | Specification>

`category` `block-prose` · Layer 1 `<glossary>` · JATS `<glossary>` · `content` `structured`

*No kwargs or boolean flags.*

Produced by: canonical `<glossary>`.

<sub-section | Examples>

<code #code:l1-glossary-1>
<glossary #project-terms>
  <glossary-entry>
    <dt | enscribe>
    <dd | An academic publishing system built on HTML+CSS+JS.>
  </glossary-entry>
  <glossary-entry>
    <dt | Layer 1>
    <dd | The canonical semantic HTML vocabulary.>
  </glossary-entry>
</glossary>
</code>

*Render pending — indented long-form closing tag not recognised → tag-error (issue #276).*

*A glossary with two entries. Each &lt;glossary-entry&gt; uses &lt;dt&gt;/&lt;dd&gt; for its term and definition (the same shapes &lt;dl&gt; uses), wrapped in the entry's own envelope for cross-reference / styling.*

</section>

<section #l1-glossary-entry | `<glossary-entry>`>

<sub-section | Specification>

`category` `block-prose` · Layer 1 `<glossary-entry>` · JATS `<def-item>` · `content` `structured`

*No kwargs or boolean flags.*

Produced by: canonical `<glossary-entry>`.

<sub-section | Examples>

<code #code:l1-glossary-entry-1>
<glossary-entry #term:enscribe>
  <dt | enscribe>
  <dd | An academic publishing system built on HTML+CSS+JS.>
</glossary-entry>
</code>

<minipage #mp:l1-glossary-entry-1>
<glossary-entry #term:enscribe>
  <dt | enscribe>
  <dd | An academic publishing system built on HTML+CSS+JS.>
</glossary-entry>
</minipage>

*A single glossary entry. The id uses the "term:" colon-prefix convention so cross-references like &lt;ref @term:enscribe&gt; can resolve into the entry.*

</section>

<section #l1-hr | `<hr>`>

<sub-section | Specification>

`category` `block-prose` · Layer 1 `<hr>` · JATS `<hr>` · `content` `none`

| attribute | kind | values / default | notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| `type` | kwarg | `scene-break` `section-break` `ornamental` `decorative` `other` | Optional classification of the thematic break's role. Affects rendering (scene breaks render as blank space; ornamental breaks render with decorative characters or images). |

Produced by: canonical `<hr>` · markdown `---`.

<sub-section | Examples>

<code #code:l1-hr-1>
<hr>
</code>

*Render pending — bare `<hr>` / `<hr type=…>` parsed as a long-form opener (no self-close) → tag-error (issue #275).*

<code #code:l1-hr-2>
<hr type=scene-break>
</code>

*Render pending — bare `<hr>` / `<hr type=…>` parsed as a long-form opener (no self-close) → tag-error (issue #275).*

<code #code:l1-hr-3>
<p | First paragraph.>

<hr type=ornamental>

<p | Second paragraph after a thematic break.>
</code>

*Render pending — bare `<hr>` / `<hr type=…>` parsed as a long-form opener (no self-close) → tag-error (issue #275).*

</section>

<section #l1-note | `<note>`>

<sub-section | Specification>

`category` `block-prose` · Layer 1 `<note>` · JATS `<fn>` · `content` `prose` → `<children>`

| attribute | kind | values / default | notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| `placement` | kwarg | `end` `foot` `side` | Determines where the note content is collected. "end" collects at article-back; "foot" collects at article-back (distinguished from "end" by CSS class on the note-list); "side" renders the content inline-adjacent to the marker. Document-wide default is "end". |
| `position` | kwarg | `end` `foot` `side` | Legacy alias for "placement" (same values, same data-note-placement output). Retained for backwards compatibility; "placement" is preferred for new documents. |
| `type` | kwarg | `substantive` `technical` `editorial` `translator` `other` | Optional classification. Most notes are substantive (authorial commentary). |

Produced by: canonical `<note>`.

<sub-section | Examples>

<code #code:l1-note-1>
Some text<note | A substantive note about the text.>.
</code>

<minipage #mp:l1-note-1>
Some text<note | A substantive note about the text.>.
</minipage>

<code #code:l1-note-2>
A claim<note placement=foot | A footnote.>.
</code>

<minipage #mp:l1-note-2>
A claim<note placement=foot | A footnote.>.
</minipage>

<code #code:l1-note-3>
A definition<note placement=side | Inline-adjacent note.>.
</code>

<minipage #mp:l1-note-3>
A definition<note placement=side | Inline-adjacent note.>.
</minipage>

</section>

<section #l1-note-list | `<note-list>`>

<sub-section | Specification>

`category` `block-prose` · Layer 1 `<note-list>` · JATS `<fn-group>` · `content` `structured`

| attribute | kind | values / default | notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| `type` | kwarg | `end-notes` `chapter-notes` `footnotes-collected` `other` |  |

Produced by: canonical `<note-list>`.

</section>

<section #l1-p | `<p>`>

<sub-section | Specification>

`category` `block-prose` · Layer 1 `<p>` · JATS `<p>` · `content` `prose` → `<children>`

| attribute | kind | values / default | notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| `type` | kwarg | `normal` `lead` `intro` `abstract` `summary` `other` | Optional classification of the paragraph's role. Mostly used for styling (lead paragraphs render larger; abstract paragraphs styled distinctly). |

Produced by: canonical `<p>`.

<sub-section | Examples>

<code #code:l1-p-1>
<p | A simple paragraph.>
</code>

<minipage #mp:l1-p-1>
<p | A simple paragraph.>
</minipage>

<code #code:l1-p-2>
A paragraph written without explicit tags.
</code>

<minipage #mp:l1-p-2>
A paragraph written without explicit tags.
</minipage>

*In most cases, paragraphs do not need to be written with explicit tags. Plain markdown handles paragraph separation: blank lines delineate paragraphs. The explicit &lt;p&gt; form is used when attributes (id, classes, type) are needed.*

<code #code:l1-p-3>
<p type=lead | The opening paragraph of an article.>
</code>

<minipage #mp:l1-p-3>
<p type=lead | The opening paragraph of an article.>
</minipage>

</section>

<section #l1-summary | `<summary>`>

<sub-section | Specification>

`category` `block-prose` · Layer 1 `<summary>` · JATS `<(no direct JATS counterpart; HTML-native)>` · `content` `prose` → `<children>`

*No kwargs or boolean flags.*

Produced by: canonical `<summary>`.

<sub-section | Examples>

<code #code:l1-summary-1>
<summary | More background>
</code>

<minipage #mp:l1-summary-1>
<summary | More background>
</minipage>

*The visible heading of a &lt;details&gt; disclosure. Appears as a child of &lt;details&gt;.*

<code #code:l1-summary-2>
<summary | Click to reveal the <em | hidden> details>
</code>

<minipage #mp:l1-summary-2>
<summary | Click to reveal the <em | hidden> details>
</minipage>

*Inline markup in a summary. The recursive-content pass parses the pipe content normally.*

</section>

